<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingnan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lingnan ('south of the ranges') is the historical name for the region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> beyond the Nanling Mountains, covering modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a> and at times northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and then ruled by the independent kingdom of Nanyue, it was organised by the Tang as a circuit governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, the base from which the Southern Han kingdom arose in the 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lingnan.jpg" alt="Lingnan" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssolbergj; derivative work by 长夜无风、玖巧仔 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanyue.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lingnan lies south of the Nanling Mountains, and covers the present-day Chinese subdivisions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, with northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, where it is called Lĩnh Nam. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> it was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> people, and the ancient Chinese court regarded it as a tropical, uncivilised land with no contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-plain-china">Zhongyuan</a>, the cradle of Chinese culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 221 to 214 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">First Emperor of Qin</a> campaigned to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingqu">Lingqu</a> canal was built to link the waterways of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the Pearl River. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 206 BCE Zhao Tuo, a Qin general serving as magistrate of the region, proclaimed himself king of Nanyue with its capital at Panyu, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, and in 180 BCE submitted as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>.</p><p>In 112 BCE the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-jia">Lü Jia</a> killed the fourth king, Zhao Xing, and his pro-Han mother, Queen Dowager Jiu. Emperor Wu of Han answered with a punitive campaign in 111 BCE that absorbed Lingnan permanently into the Han empire. The paving of the Mei Pass under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> helped the region's development.</p><p>Under the Tang the Lingnan military command, also called the Jinghai Jiedushi, was held by a long line of governors from Song Jing in 716 onwards, among them Zhang Jiugao, Li Mian and Du You. Its last holders were Liu Yin, from 901 to 911, and Liu Yan, from 911 to 917, and the region became the base of the Kingdom of Southern Han, which lasted from 917 to 971 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lingnan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liu Yan, military governor of <b>Lingnan</b> at Guangzhou in succession to his brother Liu Yin, proclaimed himself emperor of Great Yue, renaming the state Han the next year.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Liu Yin, of a family that had grown wealthy in the southern trade, became military governor of <b>Lingnan</b> at Guangzhou late in the Tang.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-han">Southern Han</a></li></ul></section>
